From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: caskade <caskade@localhost>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 10:20:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Expose ARIA states and properties to AccessKit

The AccessKit bridge mapped roles and a handful of attributes (label,
description, disabled, expanded, hidden, level, selected). Screen readers
also need the state and value of controls and how regions relate:

- checked and pressed (`aria-checked`, `aria-pressed`, native checkboxes and
  radio buttons) as `toggled`;
- `aria-current`, `aria-modal`, `aria-busy`, `aria-invalid`,
  `aria-required`, `aria-readonly`, `aria-multiselectable`,
  `aria-haspopup`, `aria-orientation`, `aria-posinset`, `aria-setsize`;
- live regions: `aria-live` and `aria-atomic`, with the implicit values of
  `alert`, `status` and `log`;
- values: `aria-valuenow`/`-valuemin`/`-valuemax`/`-valuetext`, the native
  values of `<progress>`, `<meter>` and range and number inputs, a text
  input's value (masked for passwords) and its placeholder;
- `aria-activedescendant` and `aria-controls`, resolved after every node is
  built, as `aria-labelledby` is;
- native `disabled` and `required` on form controls, and a link's URL.

`role` is read as a token list (the first known role wins), and `switch`,
`searchbox`, `separator`, `meter`, `figure`, `table`, `cell`, `paragraph`,
`feed`, `term`, `generic`, `none` and `presentation` are mapped.

A checked state or a text input's value changing now marks the tree as
changed, so the next frame sends it.
